{title: O Sacred Head, Now Wounded}
{artist: Bernard of Clairvaux; tr. James W. Alexander}
{genre: Easter}
{style: Hymn}
{flow: Meditative}
{key: G}
{tempo: 66}
{time: 4/4}
{score: o-sacred-head-now-wounded.abc}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
O [G]sacred [C]Head, now [G]wounded
With [D]grief and [Em]shame weighed [D]down
Now [G]scornfully [C]sur[G]rounded
With [Em]thorns, Thine [D]only [G]crown
How [G]pale Thou [C]art with [G]anguish
With [D]sore a[Em]buse and [D]scorn
How [G]does that [C]visage [G]languish
Which [Em]once was [D]bright as [G]morn
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
What [G]Thou, my [C]Lord, hast [G]suffered
Was [D]all for [Em]sinners' [D]gain
Mine, [G]mine was [C]the trans[G]gression
But [Em]Thine the [D]deadly [G]pain
Lo, [G]here I [C]fall, my [G]Savior
'Tis [D]I de[Em]serve Thy [D]place
Look [G]on me [C]with Thy [G]favor
Vouch[Em]safe to [D]me Thy [G]grace
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
What [G]language [C]shall I [G]borrow
To [D]thank Thee, [Em]dearest [D]friend
For [G]this Thy [C]dying [G]sorrow
Thy [Em]pity [D]without [G]end
O [G]make me [C]Thine for[G]ever
And [D]should I [Em]fainting [D]be
Lord, [G]let me [C]never, [G]never
Out[Em]live my [D]love to [G]Thee
{end_of_verse}
